A||||11||sqoach||Problem with brakes...need help!||||4||
Z||000000||sqoach||08-18-2001||06:36 PM||||My pads seem to be rubbing all the time when I'm driving. I don't know much about brakes and stuff. It seems as if the caliper piston is not releasing all the way, which is making the brake pads rub on the rotors. I'm not sure what to do. I cleaned the sliding bolts and put a thin coat of grease on them, but that didn't seem to help. How easily are those suppose to slide back and forth? I haven't bled the system yet. Should I go ahead and do that? I have the front jacked up right now, so I'm not sure if the rear brakes are rubbing too. Also, my brake pedal doesn't seem to depress very far. Probably about 3" at most. Is that normal?<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">   <P>84 SE (FOR SALE)*sale pending*<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)($50)*sale pending*<BR>86 SC (FOR SALE)<BR>86 SE V6 (new project)||||reg||4
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||08-18-2001||06:53 PM||||Hey pal you know  what is going on the pistons are probobly sticking While you have it up in the air can you turn the wheel by hand should turn fairly easily.If not you have a caliper problem.like replacement.hope this helps you out||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||08-18-2001||07:23 PM||||What kind of grease did you use.... never ever use any kind of petrolium product on brake parts.... Use only grease made specifically for brake work.<P>Is it one caliper dragging or all? All could also be the Master Cylinder or brake booster.||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||08-18-2001||09:03 PM||||The wheels don't turn very well when I spin them. It's not hard for me to turn them, but there's definately a problem. So far, both front wheels are like that. Haven't checked the rear yet. I used a multi-purpose grease. Says it's used for all kinds of automotive purposes. I think I used this same kind on the bearings, but not completely sure. Someone told me it could be the brake booster, but I have no idea what that is. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||sqoach||08-18-2001||09:32 PM||||Just checked the rears. They rub too. I put on my e-brake, then released it, and the driver's rear wheel had a hard time turning. The passenger's side was the same, still rubbing a bit. My e-brake is kinda tight. I can pull it up about 4 or 5 clicks. Should I loosen it? Seems to be enough slack in the cable for it to be ok.||||reg||1
Z||000005||sqoach||08-19-2001||12:16 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_zz.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||08-19-2001||12:23 PM||||The booster is the huge black thing that the MC is bolted to.<P>loosen the 2 nuts that hold the MC to the booster a couple turns and pull on the MC a bit. If the booster is hanging the MC then that should let the calipers off. If that doesn't work, then you've got other major problems. <P>The fact that you've used something besides brake grease liekly means trouble. Most greases swell the rubber parts. That makes them tight on the slide bolts. The rubber rings in the slide holes need to be replaced if that's happened.||||reg||1
Z||000007||sqoach||08-21-2001||01:07 AM||||I thought that grease might not be the right kind. I went and bought some brake grease and some brake cleaner. I'll get it cleaned up before anything might happen. I'm also gonna get some new brake pads for the front. I think part of the problem is crappy, cheap pads. They could be causing the "grindy squeaky" noise I've been hearing. And it couldn't hurt to put some new ones on anyway. I get a pretty good deal. I'm gonna be working quite a bit on the brakes tomorrow. I'll keep you posted on what happens.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rodmcneill||08-21-2001||02:13 AM||||Ogre, can Mobil 1 synthetic grease be used on slider bolts?||||reg||5
Z||000009||theogre||08-21-2001||06:57 PM||||NO grease is acceptable except grease made specifically for this job! It will say clearly on the label that it is made for brake work.<P>Regular Synthetic grease will cause the same swelling damage as oil based.||||reg||1
Z||000010||sqoach||08-21-2001||10:39 PM||||Well, I didn't get too far today. It seems as if the brake grease is too thick or something. Wouldn't some type of oil allow it to slide more easily? Is it suppose to slide easily? And should I bleed the brakes??||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||08-21-2001||11:10 PM||||Depending on exactly what the old grease was the rubbers may have already swollen. they will likely need to be replaced.<P>At this point I strongly suggest you find someone to look at it locally.<P>You could have problems ranging from relativly simple to seriously major going on. It's beyond any further guessing I can do w/o seeing it.||||reg||1
